//OUT="()"

// A void value carries no information, but it is a real object with a real
// address — the normalizer rewrites `void` to an empty struct, so there is
// something for `&r` to point at. Reading back through the reference gives the
// void value again, which prints as `()`.
//
// This was briefly a known-bug case asserting the opposite. That was written
// when the plan was to erase void variables in codegen, which would have left
// `&r` pointing at a variable that was never declared; the empty-struct
// lowering makes the question go away.

fn nothing
    ret

fn main
    r = nothing()
    p = &r
    out "{|p}"
